 data-driven ones. Our strategy should focus on long-term sustainability, which requires a stable hand rather than chasing short-term gains that could end up costing us dearly.  The argument that the technology sector will lead economic recovery is also overly optimistic. Yes, historically it has been a growth driver, but this time the fundamentals are different. Companies in the sector are witnessing higher costs, which could erode margins and squeeze consumer spending. Apple’s discretionary products could suffer if consumers are tightening their belts amid inflationary pressures. A conservative approach, which maintains liquidity and avoids exposure to high-risk trades, would better serve to weather these potential downturns.  Finally, while I understand the sentiment that waiting in cash may seem like a conservative approach, it provides us with flexibility. Our capital remains protected, allowing us to seize opportunities when the market presents itself under more favorable conditions—ideally at a lower point of risk rather than being overly exposed when things are uncertain.   In conclusion, optimism about recovery and market potential is fine and is part of a trader's mindset, but it must be tempered with a realistic assessment of risk. Stability and security should always be at the forefront of our strategy. Holding our position now and preserving our capital is the prudent route in an uncertain environment, setting us up for more reliable long-term growth when the market conditions improve organically.
